{
  "slug": "quick-sort",
  "title": "Quick Sort",
  "summary": "Partition around a pivot, fix its final position, then recursively sort both sides.",
  "usage": "How it works\nThis example uses Lomuto partitioning with the last element as pivot. Values <= pivot are collected before index i; after scanning, the pivot is swapped into i. Useful for learning partitioning and generally fast in-memory sorting. Sorted, reversed, or all-equal inputs can cause quadratic work and deep recursion here; randomized or three-way partitioning improves practical robustness.\n\nProperties and prerequisites\nStable: no. In-place partitioning: yes, but recursion uses stack space. For very large adversarial inputs use an iterative or depth-limited implementation.\n\nComplexity\nTime: O(n log n) average; O(n\u00b2) worst. Space: O(log n) average stack; O(n) worst.\n\nExample\nInput: [5, 2, 4, 1, 3]. Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5].\n\nTry it\nUse Play/Pause, Previous/Next, the timeline, and playback speed. Answer the prediction checkpoints to continue; feedback reveals the expected answer. Python is the line-synchronized walkthrough; Java is an equivalent complete implementation. Sorting functions accept empty arrays, duplicates, negative values, and already sorted inputs. Java methods belong inside a class; Python functions can be called directly.",
  "category": "Sorting",
  "difficulty": "MEDIUM",
  "visualizer": "ARRAY",
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n log n) average; O(n\u00b2) worst",
    "space": "O(log n) average stack; O(n) worst"
  },
  "pythonSource": "def quick_sort(a):\n    def sort(lo, hi):\n        if lo >= hi:\n            return\n        pivot = a[hi]  # Lomuto partition uses the last value as pivot.\n        i = lo\n        for j in range(lo, hi):\n            if a[j] <= pivot:\n                a[i], a[j] = a[j], a[i]  # Grow the partition of values <= pivot.\n                i += 1\n        a[i], a[hi] = a[hi], a[i]  # Put the pivot in its final position.\n        sort(lo, i - 1)\n        sort(i + 1, hi)\n    sort(0, len(a) - 1)\n    return a\n",
  "javaSource": "static void quickSort(int[] a) {\n    quickRange(a, 0, a.length - 1);\n}\nstatic void quickRange(int[] a, int lo, int hi) {\n    if (lo >= hi) return;\n    int pivot = a[hi], i = lo;\n    for (int j = lo; j < hi; j++) {\n        if (a[j] <= pivot) {\n            int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[j]; a[j] = t;\n            i++;\n        }\n    }\n    int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[hi]; a[hi] = t;\n    quickRange(a, lo, i - 1);\n    quickRange(a, i + 1, hi);\n}",
